+# Agent Note: Pre-tool input preparation
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-06-30-pre-tool-input-rewrite.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Hook-authored arguments must agree across dispatch, permission checks, concurrency classification and UI audit. Mutating `ToolExecution.arguments` after logging would execute a different command from the one displayed. Rewriting the model's authored assistant message would also obscure who changed the arguments.
+
+## Decision
+
+The registry awaits serial `tools/prepare-input` with a scoped, temporary `replaceArguments()` capability. Replacements cross lossless JSON validation and deep freezing. Schedulers resolve each input once before classification and audit logging, then pass the sealed execution to existing policy, guards and dispatch. A call held at an exclusive barrier retains its prepared input without rerunning hooks; live concurrency classification still runs before admission.
+
+The authored assistant message remains immutable. A sourced `tool-input-rewrite` correction carries original and accepted arguments as JSON text after the final tool result. JSON text supports parameter trees deeper than structured cloning can handle. Corrections survive denial, cancellation and result replacement; PTC ferries nested correction context through its outer result. Native `tool/call.arguments` retains the model-authored bytes required for released-session pairing. Optional `effectiveArguments` records accepted input; current presentation readers prefer it. PTC dispatch records already use accepted arguments. Older presentation clients that ignore the optional field show the original input.
+
+The Claude bridge validates `PreToolUse.updatedInput` against the visible tool schema before applying it. It stores the same invocation's decision by execution token for ordinary pre-policy, so hooks execute once. `allow` still delegates to ordinary policy and monotonic guards; `ask` presents accepted arguments. Invalid replacement input denies dispatch. Other hook events do not rewrite input.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+Mutating published execution arguments would disagree with audit readers and permit policy to observe different input. Replacing the assistant message would attribute hook changes to the model. Keeping preparation separate preserves both authorship and the existing immutable policy interface. The [interception decision](2026-06-30-interception-extension-points.md) continues to own the later waterfall phases.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Hook audit events may precede `tool/call`. A changed call adds a model-visible correction and therefore consumes additional context tokens without modifying the reusable history prefix. Input preparation is serial; it can delay admission while an earlier parallel call runs. Hook concurrency and unsupported bridge handler kinds remain separate concerns.
