+### HTTP handlers
+
+A handler with `type: "http"` posts the event payload as JSON to `url`. Optional `headers`, `allowedEnvVars` and `timeout` configure the request. Only a 2xx response with valid hook JSON can change execution; an empty response means `{}`. HTTP status codes never use shell exit-code rules. Plain response text, malformed JSON, non-2xx responses, redirects, connection errors and timeouts produce nonblocking diagnostics. Requests do not follow redirects; cancellation covers response-body reading and discards late decisions. An `async: true` response envelope has no synchronous control effect.
+
+Headers expand uppercase `$VAR` and `${VAR}` only when the handler permits the name and every configured host variable allowlist permits it. Known ordinary credential variables additionally need explicit host approval; CLI-internal credential names remain withheld. Missing or disallowed variables become empty text, and CR, LF and NUL are removed. Direct connections reject the CLI’s private and link-local address ranges, including mixed public/private DNS answers; loopback remains allowed. Proxied requests use the installed host proxy policy and leave target resolution to that proxy. Configure host allowlists on the bridge, not in untrusted agent frontmatter.
+
+### Local agent definitions
+
+`installClaudeAgentHooks` mounts parsed frontmatter directly in an unpublished child's scope. The spawn provider validates local definitions before publication, skips untrusted project hooks, and ignores plugin-agent hooks. User-agent hooks are trusted; project-agent hooks require `projects[exactProjectDirectory].hasTrustDialogAccepted: true` in the execution world's `~/.claude.json`. Ancestor-directory trust does not grant this permission. Invalid local event maps, matchers, timeouts, or unsupported handler types reject agent setup. See the [spawn provider](../../subagent/subagent-spawn-in-process/README.md).
+
+Local `PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, and `PostToolUseFailure` run only for the child. Local `Stop` entries become `SubagentStop`, match the declared agent name, and can force another child step or halt it with `continue: false`. `stop_hook_active` is true after a hook has forced continuation in the same turn; `last_assistant_message` carries the latest assistant text. Handler effects, active commands and HTTP requests end with the child. Session, prompt, and subagent lifecycle observers are only installed by file-based configuration.
+
+`continue: false` halts the active prompt, tool sequence, or stopping turn and takes precedence over blocking feedback or forced continuation. A pre-tool stop prevents dispatch; a post-tool stop preserves the final tool result before cancelling further work. `stopReason` is recorded as the hook cancellation reason, defaulting to `Execution stopped by hook` when absent or empty. It does not arm a halt for later user turns. `SessionStart` and detached lifecycle observers do not halt runs.
+
+`PreToolUse.updatedInput` is validated against the visible tool schema and applied before audit logging, concurrency classification, approval and execution guards. Invalid input denies dispatch and ignores the remaining fields of that hook output. A deny/block output does not apply its replacement. Extra-key validation errors are ignored, matching the CLI validator; ordinary tool validation still runs. The authored assistant call stays unchanged while a durable correction carries original and accepted JSON. `allow` delegates to normal policy; `ask` requests approval of accepted arguments. Other events do not rewrite input.
+
 ### How hooks run and fail
 
-- Hooks run in your project directory — the agent's session workspace — so `pwd` and relative paths in your hooks refer to your project, not the server's launch directory.
+Successful `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` stdout that does not start with `{` becomes logged model context, trimmed and framed as `<system-reminder>\n<Event[:matcher]> hook success: <text>\n</system-reminder>`. Other events require structured `additionalContext`. Empty stdout and unsuccessful, cancelled, or timed-out output add no plain context; cancelled and timed-out runs discard control fields even when a signal handler exits zero.
+
+- Command hooks run in your project directory — the agent's session workspace — so `pwd` and relative paths in your hooks refer to your project, not the server's launch directory.
+- Configured command hooks run as trusted host code outside the agent's file sandbox, including when the agent is read-only. They retain shell credential scrubbing, cancellation and timeouts. Register only trusted commands; local-agent definition trust checks still apply.
 - `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` and `${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}` in command strings are replaced from your config, and `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` is set for every hook process.
 - One config applies to the whole process: it is read once at startup, and a relative `configPath` resolves from the directory that launched the process.
 - Hooks on the same event run one after another, in config order.
