 <a id="use-this-package"></a>
 ## Use this package
 
+This provider supports `agentOptions.streamToolCalls: true`. Complete tool blocks are recorded and executed while the model response remains open. The caller must opt in for each one-shot child; the option is not inherited from its parent. Request retry and transport fallback remain separate policies.
+
+agentType resolves declared names in nested project-ancestor/user .claude/agents Markdown and enabled plugin namespaces, including custom manifest files. Project/user definitions require name and description; plugin files may use the filename. tools and disallowedTools split comma- or space-separated entries outside parentheses, normalize Claude aliases and argument-bearing tool names before intersecting caller restrictions, ignore unavailable definition selectors, and reject explicit lists that resolve to zero tools. Plugin permissionMode, hooks and mcpServers are ignored, matching Claude plugin-agent rules. Declared skills preload registry or .claude/skills bodies into logged child-only instruction messages, retaining resource bases; missing or disabled skills warn and skip. Exact skill names precede the agent plugin namespace and other plugin suffix matches. Each call rereads disk definitions. Agent worktree isolation applies when the caller omits isolation. worktree isolation creates a detached Git worktree, removes unchanged trees on disposal, and retains edits, untracked files or new commits. Bash clamps parse every command in lists and pipelines, match exact/prefix/wildcard rules against both raw and resolved spans, and permit literal quoting and statically verified redirects. Unknown expansions, compound control syntax, parse errors and commands longer than 10,000 characters are rejected. The grammar loads before child publication; each guarded call releases its parser and syntax tree. Clamps also disable alternate execution and MCP tools. These options never widen the parent sandbox/approval pin.
+
 Mount this backend in a composition that delegates work to fresh in-process children. The common path is explicit: load the subagent service and this backend, then point a delegation tool such as `dsh-tool-subagent` at the `spawn` provider.
 
+Agent definitions also supply effort and maxTurns. Effort accepts Claude named levels, the med alias and integer values, then passes the normalized identifier to the selected Lab provider; unsupported provider efforts fail explicitly. Caller effort overrides the definition, which overrides inherited parent effort. A valid positive maxTurns limits model iterations, including hook-driven continuation, after admitted tools finish. The child retains its last output with stopReason=max-turns; normal completion on the final permitted iteration remains completed. Invalid metadata warns and is omitted.
+
+Tool-list arguments select the whole named tool: `tools: Bash(echo:*)` admits Bash, and `disallowedTools: Bash(echo:*)` removes Bash entirely. They do not create command permissions. Use the separate `bashCommandClamp` option for command restrictions. Clamp entries decode escaped parentheses and backslashes, require nonempty unpadded content, and reject `Bash(*)` because the CLI parses it as a bare tool rather than a command rule. `Bash(**)` is an explicit all-command pattern; the normal parser, sandbox, permission and caller restrictions still apply. An `Agent(type)` allow selector remains unavailable until subtype enforcement exists; a disallowed entry removes the whole Agent tool.
+
+Local agent mcpServers entries accept a mounted server name or a one-key inline stdio/HTTP configuration. Mounted names borrow the parent-visible connection, tools, resources and instructions without transferring ownership. Other names search the exact project record in ~/.claude.json, nearest valid ancestor .mcp.json, then the user mcpServers record. Project files require enabledMcpjsonServers or enableAllProjectMcpServers; disabledMcpjsonServers wins. User settings may grant approval; project and local settings may grant it only for an explicitly trusted directory. Each start rereads bounded disk files. Disk command, args, env, URL and headers expand ${NAME} and ${NAME:-default} once; empty values stay empty and missing variables retain their placeholder with a warning. Inline fields remain literal.
+
+New clients require a local execution world. Inline clients also require a trusted project definition or user definition. Connections start inside unpublished setup with a ten-second deadline, and child disposal drains their processes. Lookup uses the original project while stdio runs in the selected worktree. MCP tools are added after ordinary definition tools selection, while caller allow/deny rules, disallowedTools, plan and Bash clamps still apply. Shared resource helpers follow ordinary definition and caller selection, including the ListMcpResources/ReadMcpResource aliases and their Tool-suffixed names. Borrowing intersects source resource admission, Bash clamps hide shared resource helpers, and filtered helpers are absent from schemas, PTC bindings and resource guidance. List changes and reconnects retain admission rules. Invalid or failed connections warn and skip; an explicit tools list with no surviving tools rejects startup.
+
 ### When to choose it
 
 Choose the spawn backend when the child needs no parent conversation and running in this process is acceptable. Avoid it when the child must build on completed parent turns — the fork backend seeds that history — or when the child must run outside this process, which the out-of-process backends provide. Because the child inherits the parent's working directory and LLM selection by default, a self-contained prompt behaves exactly as written.
